;Match details

* '''Match 1: Ishida def. Tanaka.''' The veteran's resurgence continues. Tanaka's brawling took the fight to the floor; Ishida's leg-work was the answer back in the ring, and the Ishida Lock at 12:39 made it four submissions of the tournament. Ishida is 4-4, 8 points, a winning record now live with Taumafa remaining in Round 9. Tanaka falls to 1-7. '''Crowd:''' '''ISHIDA''' chants. The Tsuda crowd has adopted the resurrection completely.

* '''Match 2: Ono def. Diallo.''' The young ace against the Hammer. Diallo's traditional Asikyran wrestling controlled the opening; Ono's speed turned it, and the bridging German suplex at 11:52 — the strength nobody credits to the block's youngest body — ended it. Ono is 4-4, 8 points; Diallo falls to 2-6. '''Crowd:''' '''ONO''' chants. The passing of the guard waits another year, but the young man's G1 debut has been the block's quiet pleasure.

* '''Match 3: Kuroki def. Jusuf.''' The Kuroshio enforcer against the truth-teller — and the statement. Kuroki, 4-3 and needing the win to stay alive, absorbed the kneebar attempt and answered with the Black Iron lariat at 14:07. Kuroki is 5-3, 10 points; Jusuf falls to 5-3, and the block's quiet third is now tied with the Dragon one point behind the leaders. '''Crowd:''' '''KUROKI''' chants. BOOS for Jusuf's walk-out, which came without acknowledgment.

* '''Match 4: Reyes def. KAZUKI.''' The shock of the tournament. KAZUKI, 6-2 and sliding, opened with the full arsenal; Reyes, 0-7 and fighting for a first win, absorbed everything and countered the Twin Star Press into the Cobra Clutch at 16:33. The Shooting Star tapped. Reyes is 1-7, 2 points, off the floor at last; KAZUKI falls to 6-3, and the man who led the block for five rounds is now tied with the man at the bottom in the Tsuda crowd's memory. '''Crowd:''' '''REYES''' chants — the loudest pop of the night, and the Cobra stood in the ring long after the bell, letting it land. Respect applause for KAZUKI, who bowed and walked out first.

* '''Match 5: Takagi def. Taumafa.''' The main event — the block's marquee collision, and the Dragon's answer. Taumafa, 6-1 and leading on points, took the fight to Takagi with everything the family does; the Dragon's lariat at 22:41 — the move that ended Jusuf's undefeated run and KAZUKI's — ended the family giant's second G1 loss. Clean pin. Takagi is 6-1, 12 points, tied with KAZUKI one point behind Taumafa, with the head-to-head over the giant now his. Taumafa falls to 6-2, and the block's final night in Toyozawa is now a three-man race. '''Crowd:''' '''TAKAGI''' chants. GASPS at the lariat. STANDING OVATION for both men — the Tsuda crowd knows what it just watched.

